.TH MIRI_SDR 1 "October 2026" "libmirisdr-6" "User Commands"
.ad l
.ss 12 0
.SH NAME
miri_sdr \- capture samples from a Mirics based receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B miri_sdr
.B \-f
.I frequency
.RI [ options ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B miri_sdr
tunes a receiver built on the Mirics MSi2500 and MSi001, such as the SDRplay
RSP1, RSP1B, RSPdx\-R2 and RSPduo, and writes the samples it receives to a file.
Give
.B \-
as the file to write them to standard output.
It runs until it is stopped with Ctrl+C.
.PP
Complex samples are written as I and Q pairs, little endian.
With
.B "\-m 504"
each value is a signed 8 bit number. The other formats give signed 16 bit
numbers. With
.B \-B
the output is 32 bit floats.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " frequency"
The frequency to tune to, in Hz.
.TP
.BI \-s " rate"
The sample rate in Hz. The default is 2048000.
.TP
.BI \-m " format"
How samples are packed on the USB.
.B auto
(the default) takes the format with the most bits that the USB can carry at the rate.
.B 504
is 8 bits,
.B 384
10 bits plus 2,
.B 336
12 bits and
.B 252
14 bits.
The real formats use one converter, and only make sense with a low IF:
.BR autor ,
.B 768r
(10 bits plus 2),
.B 672r
(12 bits) and
.B 504r
(16 bits).
.TP
.BI \-g " gain"
The total gain in units of 10 dB, so 4.5 is 45 dB. The nearest gain the band has
is taken. Without it the gain stays as it is set. There is no automatic gain.
.TP
.BI \-G " mixer,lna,mixbuffer,baseband"
The gain of each stage instead of a total. The mixer and LNA take 0 or 1, the
mixbuffer 0, 6, 12, 18 or 24 and the baseband 0 to 59.
.TP
.BI \-i " if"
The IF in Hz: 0 (zero IF, the default), 450000, 1620000 or 2048000.
.TP
.BI \-w " bandwidth"
The IF filter in Hz. It defaults to the widest the IF allows. 200000 and 300000
need the 450 kHz IF, 600000 any low IF and 1536000 zero IF or the 1620 or
2048 kHz IF. Zero IF also takes 5000000 to 8000000 in 1 MHz steps, and 14000000
for no filter. A filter the IF cannot have is refused.
.TP
.B \-B
Write complex floats with the tuned frequency at 0 Hz, whatever the IF. A low IF
gives four times the IF, halved 0 to 8 times, for example
.BR "\-i 2048000 \-s 256000" .
With a real format one converter is used.
.TP
.BI \-D " mode"
Bypass the decimation stage.
.B auto
(the default) bypasses it above 14.5 Msps, where the PLL runs out.
.B on
always bypasses it, for 2.6 to 30 Msps.
.B off
never does, for 1.3 to 15 Msps.
.TP
.BI \-e " mode"
The USB transfer.
.B ISOC
(the default) reserves 3 packets of 1024 bytes each microframe, 24.576 MB/s.
.B ISOC2
reserves 2 and
.B ISOC1
reserves 1.
.B BULK
is not tied to microframes. 1 and 2 are taken as ISOC and BULK.
.TP
.BI \-d " index"
Which receiver to open, counting from 0. The default is 0.
.TP
.BI \-T " board"
The board, when the USB ids do not tell it: 0 a plain MSi2500 and MSi001 board,
1 the RSP1, 2 the RSP1B, 3 the RSPdx\-R2 and 4 the RSPduo.
.TP
.BI \-b " size"
The size of each block written, in bytes. The default is 16 times 16384. With
.B \-B
blocks are written as they come.
.TP
.B \-q
Swap I and Q. With a real format this picks the converter that is sampled.
.TP
.B \-S
Read synchronously instead of with callbacks. This needs the BULK transfer and
does not work with
.BR \-B .
.SH EXAMPLES
Capture 2 Msps at 100 MHz with 40 dB of gain:
.PP
.RS
miri_sdr \-f 100000000 \-s 2000000 \-g 4 capture.bin
.RE
.PP
Write baseband floats at 256 ksps through the 2048 kHz IF to another program:
.PP
.RS
miri_sdr \-f 433920000 \-i 2048000 \-s 256000 \-B \- | other_program
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/60\-libmirisdr5.rules
Lets users open the receivers without root.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR miri_fm (1),
.BR miri_eeprom (1)
.PP
https://github.com/BertoldVdb/libmirisdr\-6
